  "afd": {
    "available": true,
    "issued_at": "2026-08-31T06:38:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "af1a03e8-74a0-4249-bcf8-45f4564addca",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 310638\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1138 PM PDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Isolated-to-scattered thunderstorm activity is expected over \n  northwest Arizona today, some of which may lead to flash flooding.\n\n* Dry and relatively cooler conditions are on track for most areas \n  through the rest of the workweek.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Saturday.\n\nThe primary forecast concern for today is heavy rainfall potential \nover northwestern Arizona. The current pattern aloft includes high \npressure over the southern half of the United States and a trough \noff the Pacific Coast. Southwesterly flow between these two features \nmaintains dry conditions for most of the region west of the Colorado \nRiver. However, ensembles indicate between 1.0 and 1.6 inch of PWAT \nover northwestern Arizona as well as eastern Lincoln and Clark \ncounties. At the same time, a shortwave will push through the Great \nBasin, providing a boost of instability and lift. High resolution \nguidance shows upwards of 2400 J/kg SBCAPE over Mohave County \ncoinciding with the shortwave passage. Between instability and \nmoisture, robust storms with efficient rain rates leading to flash \nflooding is the main concern. Issued a Flood Watch for this morning \nthrough the evening for northern Mohave County where the flood \nthreat appears the greatest. Looking ahead, the trough may be able \nto draw moisture from Hurricane Karina, but forecast soundings show \nit mainly in the form of high cloud cover, if it makes it at all. \nAlso watching for another potential push of tropical moisture at the \nend of the week, but confidence remains low at this time. \n\nBesides precipitation, southwesterly flow and enhanced 850 mb winds \nbetween 30 and 40 knots will maintain gusty surface winds through \nmost of the week. The strongest gusts will be over high terrain in \nthe Mojave Desert, generally peaking between 35 and 40 mph. \nTemperatures should also bottom out on Wednesday with highs around 5 \ndegrees below normal for this time of year.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...South-\nsouthwest breezes continue throughout the TAF period, with gusts \npicking up by mid/late morning and persisting through sunset. Peak \ngusts 20-30 knots. Some uncertainty Monday evening as outflow from \nstorms in northwestern AZ tries to reach the valley. If it does (30% \nchance), expect an easterly or northeasterly wind shift. Few to \nscattered clouds above 10kft.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light winds tonight give \nway to another round of southerly to westerly breezes on Monday. \nAfternoon gusts generally 15-30 knots, waning in most locations \naround sunset. Isolated to scattered convection anticipated in \nMohave County and eastern Lincoln County, posing the risk of strong \noutflow winds and heavy rain. Will have to see if thunderstorm \noutflow can reach sites in the lower Colorado River Valley (50% \nchance) and the Las Vegas Valley (30% chance) Monday evening. \nPrecipitation chances begin to wane after sunset. Isolated to \nscattered clouds at or above 10kft in most locations, with scattered \nto broken conditions where convective coverage is greatest.\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Meltzer\nAVIATION...Woods\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
